# Line endings.
#
# The repository is developed on Windows and deployed on Ubuntu. Most files do
# not care, but the ones below break in ways that are hard to read if they
# arrive on the server with CRLF:
#
#   * a shell script with CRLF fails as "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M",
#     which does not mention line endings at all;
#   * systemd reads a unit with CRLF and takes the carriage return as part of
#     the value, so ExecStart points at a binary whose name ends in ^M;
#   * nginx and logrotate are more forgiving but not reliably so.
#
# These are copied to the server from a working tree, so what is checked out on
# Windows is what lands on Linux. Pin them to LF and the question never arises.

*.sh            text eol=lf
*.service       text eol=lf
*.socket        text eol=lf
*.timer         text eol=lf
*.conf          text eol=lf
deploy/logrotate/*  text eol=lf
